<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Hardened-runtime entitlements. Notarization requires the hardened runtime,
  and the hardened runtime breaks a PyInstaller onefile binary unless these
  are granted, so these exist for sidecar/dist/pictoria-sidecar rather than
  for the Rust app, which needs none of them.

  A PyInstaller onefile build is a self-extracting archive: at launch it
  unpacks its Python interpreter, .so modules and .dylibs into a fresh
  /var/folders/.../_MEIxxxxxx directory and dlopen()s them from there. Under
  the hardened runtime that is exactly the behaviour library validation is
  designed to stop, because those extracted libraries are not sealed into any
  signature the loader trusts.

  disable-library-validation
      The load-bearing one. Without it every dlopen() of an extracted .dylib
      is refused and the sidecar dies during startup, after notarization has
      already succeeded, so it fails in the user's hands rather than in CI.

  allow-dyld-environment-variables
      The bootloader re-executes itself with DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH pointing at the
      extraction directory; the hardened runtime strips DYLD_* unless allowed.

  allow-unsigned-executable-memory
      torch and numpy allocate writable-executable pages for their dispatch
      and kernel paths.

  Scoped to what the frozen Python runtime needs. Nothing here widens the
  app's own attack surface beyond loading libraries we shipped ourselves, and
  none of it is App Sandbox: this is Developer ID distribution, not the App
  Store, so no com.apple.security.app-sandbox key belongs in this file.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-dyld-environment-variables</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
